<BlogPost slug="how-to-watch-tv-without-cable-canada">
  <p>Cutting the cord in Canada comes down to three things: a stable internet connection, a device that can stream, and a service to watch. Here is the simple path.</p>

  <h2>1. Check your internet</h2>
  <p>You need about 5 Mbps per screen for SD, 10 for HD, 15 for FHD and 25 or more for 4K. Add them up for the screens that run at once. Our <a href="/iptv-internet-speed-requirements/">speed guide</a> has the full table.</p>

  <h2>2. Pick a device</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Smart TV.</strong> Install a player app from the TV's app store.</li>
    <li><strong>Firestick or Fire TV.</strong> A popular, low-cost option. See the <a href="/setup/firestick/">Firestick guide</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Android TV box, Apple TV or Roku.</strong> All have <a href="/setup/">setup guides</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Phone, tablet or computer.</strong> Good for travel and second screens.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Choose what to watch</h2>
  <p>Many people mix a few streaming apps with one live-TV service. Prime IPTV covers live channels, sports, PPV and on-demand movies and series in one subscription, so you rely on fewer apps.</p>

  <h2>4. Test before you commit</h2>
  <p>Start the <a href="/free-trial/">24-hour free trial</a>, add the login to your player and test the channels you watch most. Choose a plan only if it works on your connection.</p>

  <h2>Quick answers</h2>
  <h3>Do I need a cable box?</h3>
  <p>No. A device you already own is usually enough.</p>
  <h3>Will live sports work?</h3>
  <p>Sports are included, and a wired connection helps at peak times. Test a live event during your trial.</p>
</BlogPost>
